    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the net displacement in both the north-south direction and the east-west direction, and then use the Pythagorean theorem to find the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the net displacement in the north-south direction.\nThe traveler first went 18 miles north and then 6 miles south. The net displacement in the north-south direction is:\n18 miles north - 6 miles south = 12 miles north.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the net displacement in the east-west direction.\nThe traveler first went 11 miles west and then 6 miles east. The net displacement in the east-west direction is:\n11 miles west - 6 miles east = 5 miles west.\n\nStep 3: Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position.\nThe straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of 12 miles north and 5 miles west. Using the Pythagorean theorem (a² + b² = c²), we can find the distance:\n(12 miles)² + (5 miles)² = c²\n144 miles² + 25 miles² = c²\n169 miles² = c²\nc = √169 miles\nc = 13 miles\n\nSo, the traveler was 13 miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
