    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve this problem. The traveler's movement forms a right triangle, with the north-south movement forming one leg and the east-west movement forming the other leg. The distance from the starting point is the hypotenuse of this right triangle.\n\nLet's calculate the total north-south distance and the total east-west distance:\n\nNorth-south distance: 18 miles (north) + 6 miles (south) = 24 miles\nEast-west distance: 11 miles (west) + 6 miles (east) = 17 miles\n\nNow we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the distance from the starting point:\n\nDistance^2 = North-south distance^2 + East-west distance^2\nDistance^2 = 24^2 + 17^2\nDistance^2 = 576 + 289\nDistance^2 = 865\n\nNow we take the square root of both sides to find the distance:\n\nDistance = √865 ≈ 29.4 miles\n\nSo, the traveler was approximately 29.4 miles from the starting point after the four legs of the journey.",
