      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by visualizing the traveler's journey on a coordinate plane. We can place the starting point at the origin, (0, 0). The traveler first heads 18 miles north, so we move up 18 units to (0, 18). Then, the traveler heads 11 miles west, so we move left 11 units to (-11, 18). Next, the traveler heads 6 miles south, so we move down 6 units to (-11, 12). Finally, the traveler heads 6 miles east, so we move right 6 units to (-5, 12).\n\nNow, we need to find the distance between the starting point (0, 0) and the final position (-5, 12). We can use the distance formula to find this distance:\n\nDistance = √((x2 - x1)² + (y2 - y1)²)\n\nPlugging in the coordinates, we get:\n\nDistance = √((-5 - 0)² + (12 - 0)²)\nDistance = √(25 + 144)\nDistance = √169\nDistance = 13\n\nSo, the traveler was 13 miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
